<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jovenel Moïse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1968">1968</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jovenel Moïse was a Haitian businessman and banana exporter who was President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> from February 2017 until his death. His term was marked by protests over corruption and fuel prices, rule by decree after parliament's mandate lapsed in 2020, and the growing power of armed gangs. He was assassinated at his home near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> on 7 July 2021 by a group of mostly Colombian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jovenel-moise.jpg" alt="Jovenel Moïse" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 總統府 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jovenel_Mo%C3%AFse_in_Taiwan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1988 drew 4% of voters, Aristide was elected in 1990 and overthrown in 1991, re-elected in 2000 with 92% on an opposition boycott and driven out in 2004; Michel Martelly left in 2016 without a successor, <b>Jovenel Moïse</b> won on a 17% turnout and was assassinated in 2021.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
